* Yin et al. (2025) H. Yin, Y. Ren, K. Yan, S. Ding, and Y. Hao ROD-mllm: towards more reliable object detection in multimodal large language models. In Proceedings of the Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Conference, pp. 14358–14368. Cited by: cite88†Appendix B .\nL175:   * Zhang et al. (2025) Y. Zhang, W. Zhang, Z. Zeng, K. Jiang, J. Li, W. Min, W. Luo, Q. Guan, J. Lin, and W. Yu MapReader: a framework for learning a visual language model for map analysis. International Journal of Geographical Information Science, pp. 1–36. Cited by: cite78†§2 .\nL176: ## Appendix A Synthetic Data Generation Details\nL177: \nL178: This appendix provides details of the assets, prompts, and algorithms used in our synthetic pipeline.\nL179: ### A.1 Map Categories\nL180: \nL181: We defined a diverse set of categories to generate representative map environments:\nL182: \nL183:     MAP_CATEGORIES = [\nL184:         \"zoo\",\nL185:         \"urban\",\nL186:         \"botanical garden\",\nL187:         \"museum\",\nL188:         \"amusement park\",\nL189:         \"national park\",\nL190:         \"hospital\",\nL191:         \"hotel\",\nL192:         \"airport\",\nL193:         \"shopping mall\",\nL194:         \"restaurant\",\nL195:         \"campus\"\nL196:     ]\nL197: ### A.2 Prompt Templates\nL198: \nL199: We employed LLMs both to generate map descriptions and to filter low-quality masks and paths. Below we include the key prompts used in the pipeline.\nL200: \nL201: #### Map Description Generator.\nL202: \nL203:     Create a short description of a top-view 2D pathfinding map of a {{ map_type }}.\nL204:     The description should be under 100 words and specify map style, landmarks,\nL205:     and key routes. The map should not have a title.\nL206: #### Mask Critic.\nL207: \nL208:     Your goal is to evaluate a segmentation mask’s accuracy in identifying paths\nL209:     based on a source image.\nL210: \nL211:     You will be given two images:\nL212:     * ‘[Image 1: Source]‘ is the original map image.\nL213:     * ‘[Image 2: Mask]‘ is the segmentation mask where path/target areas should be\nL214:       marked in white.\nL215: \nL216:     **Evaluation Criteria:**\nL217: \nL218:     * **Target Areas (Considered ’Correct’):** Any type of map paths: paved\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMapTrace: Scalable Data Generation for Route Tracing on Maps (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.19609v1)\nciteturn31893view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.19609v1\",\"pattern\":\"83%\"}); Total lines: 364\nL90: Method  | Google  | Mall  | Museum  | Nat. Park  | Theme  | Trail  | Campus  | Urban  | Zoo  | Avg.  | SR (%)\nL91: Baseline\nL92: Mask-Method  | 0.91  | 0.75  | 0.67  | 0.91  | 0.68  | 0.65  | 0.64  | 0.78  | 0.90  | 0.79  | 73.4\nL93: Pretrained Models\nL94: Gemini 2.5 Pro  | 1.25  | 0.87  | 1.16  | 1.18  | 1.57  | 1.06  | 1.13  | 1.34  | 0.80  | 1.19  | 90.6\nL95: GLM4.5V  | 2.34  | 10.79  | 3.06  | 0.71  | 2.42  | 1.83  | 0.51  | 5.20  | 1.25  | 2.30  | 70.7\nL96: QwenVL-2.5 72B  | 2.12  | 0.73  | 0.90  | 1.01  | 1.19  | 1.34  | 1.05  | 0.81  | 1.71  | 1.32  | 42.9\nL97: Gemma3-27B  | 1.60  | 0.88  | 1.51  | 1.44  | 1.63  | 1.22  | 0.69  | 0.97  | 1.16  | 1.29  | 88.3\nL98: Gemini 2.5 Flash  | 1.20  | 0.77  | 0.84  | 1.81  | 1.71  | 1.28  | 0.88  | 1.84  | 1.30  | 1.29  | 98.1\nL99: Finetuned on MapTrace (ours)\nL100: Gemma3-27B*  | 1.36_{-0.24}  | 1.03_{+0.15}  | 1.02_{-0.49}  | 1.18_{-0.26}  | 1.30_{-0.33}  | 1.00_{-0.22}  | 0.83_{+0.14}  | 1.53_{+0.56}  | 1.13_{-0.03}  | 1.13_{-0.16}  | 94.7_{+6.4}\nL101: Gemini 2.5 Flash*  | 1.14_{-0.06}  | 0.74_{-0.03}  | 0.71_{-0.13}  | 0.93_{-0.88}  | 1.04_{-0.67}  | 0.64_{-0.64}  | 0.62_{-0.26}  | 0.93_{-0.91}  | 0.90_{-0.40}  | 0.87_{-0.42}  | 98.3_{+0.2}\nL102: Table 1: Performance on MapBench. Results are reported using Normalized Dynamic Time Warping (NDTW; lower is better) and Success Rate (SR; higher is better). Asterisks (*) denote models finetuned on our synthetic dataset.\nL103: \nL104:  | Setting  | Items  | Accuracy  | False Positive Rate\nL105:  | Path Critic  | 120  | 76%  | 8%\nL106: Mask Critic  | 200  | 83%  | 9%\nL107: \nL108: Table 2: Critic audits.\nL109: ## 4 MapTrace: Synthetic Data Generation\nL110: Since collecting large-scale, pixel-accurate map annotations is prohibitively expensive, we design a fully synthetic pipeline to generate both maps and path traces automatically. Figure cite68†2 illustrates the process, which consists of four stages: (i) map image generation, (ii) path mask extraction, (iii) pixel-graph construction, and (iv) final path generation.\nL111: Each stage produces intermediate visualizations that allow MLLMs to act as “critics,” filtering low-quality samples and ensuring dataset quality.\nL112: Map Image Generation. We begin by prompting an LLM with diversified descriptions of map types (e.g., zoos, museums, amusement parks, shopping malls) and aspect ratios (details in Appendix cite18†A.1 ). These descriptions are instantiated into stylized map images using a text-to-image model. This step produces visually diverse maps that resemble real-world wayfinding designs, adhering to the language model’s layout commonsense, while remaining annotation-free.\nL113: Path Mask Generation. To identify traversable regions, we extract dominant colors from the generated image and convert them into candidate binary masks. We use $k$-means clustering in RGB pixels of the map to segment the image into $k$ color clusters (details in Appendix cite23†A.3 ). Each cluster corresponds to a candidate path region, which is evaluated by a Mask Critic LLM. Color-masks judged as valid are merged to form the final traversable path mask.\nL124: These results highlight that synthetic supervision consistently boosts robustness and substantially reduces path alignment error. Notably, some apparent regressions in NDTW arise because models now solve a larger fraction of harder queries: higher success rates expand coverage to challenging cases that pretrained models failed to attempt, which inflates average distance despite overall gains. MapTrace provides a scalable way to instill spatial reasoning that pretraining alone does not yield.\nL125: Critics Audits. While LLM-based critics inevitably introduce some judgment noise, we empirically verify that their errors do not meaningfully degrade dataset quality. We conduct targeted manual audits of both critics: We manually review 120 path critic decisions across 56 randomly sampled maps. The critic achieves 76% accuracy with only an 8% false-positive rate (i.e., truly invalid paths marked as GOOD).\nL126: The dominant error modes are (i) misclassifying background regions as traversable when background colors resemble path colors, and (ii) overlooking thin valid paths embedded within larger open regions. We manually inspect 200 mask critic judgments over 20 maps. The critic achieves 83% accuracy with a 9% false-positive rate.\nL127: Typical mistakes include (i) background pixels being included due to color similarity with accurate paths, (ii) minor non-path elements such as text being absorbed into an otherwise correct mask, and (iii) thin but valid paths occasionally labeled as invalid.\nL128: Human Inspection. To evaluate the quality of the synthetic data we sampled 200 data points for human inspection across three categories: High Quality, Acceptable, Low Quality. Annotators judged 58% of samples as high quality and only 18% as low quality, with moderate agreement (Cohen’s $\\kappa=0.36$). Details are in Appendix cite35†E .\nL129: ## 7 Conclusion\nL130: In this work, we introduce a new method for evaluating map understanding in MLLMs: models must generate an exact sequence of coordinates that forms a valid path between two points while remaining within traversable regions. We show that this task is challenging even for state-of-the-art systems.\nL147:   * Dong et al. (2025) Q. Dong, L. Dong, X. Zhang, Z. Sui, and F. Wei Self-boosting large language models with synthetic preference data. In The Thirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations, External Links: cite82†Link†openreview.net Cited by: cite77†§2 .\nL148:   * Fang et al. (2024) B. Fang, Z. Yang, S. Wang, and X. Di TraveLLM: could you plan my new public transit route in face of a network disruption?. arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.14926. Cited by: cite78†§2 .\nL149:   * [9] D. Fu, R. Guo, G. Khalighinejad, O. Liu, B. Dhingra, D. Yogatama, R. Jia, and W. Neiswanger IsoBench: benchmarking multimodal foundation models on isomorphic representations. In First Conference on Language Modeling, Cited by: cite76†§2 .\nL150:   * Jin et al. (2024) S. Jin, Y. Wang, S. Liu, Y. Zhang, and W. Gu Optimizing dataset creation: a general purpose data filtering system for training large language models. Cited by: cite77†§2 .\nL151:   * Kalervo et al. (2019) A. Kalervo, J. Ylioinas, M. Häikiö, A. Karhu, and J. Kannala Cubicasa5k: a dataset and an improved multi-task model for floorplan image analysis. In Scandinavian Conference on Image Analysis, pp. 28–40. Cited by: cite78†§2 , cite83†§3 .\nL152:   * Kamath et al. (2023) A. Kamath, J. Hessel, and K. Chang What’s “up” with vision-language models? investigating their struggle with spatial reasoning. In Proceedings of the 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pp. 9161–9175. Cited by: cite75†§1 , cite76†§2 .\nL153:   * Li et al. (2024a) C. Li, C. Zhang, S. Teufel, R. S. Doddipatla, and S. Stoyanchev Semantic map-based generation of navigation instructions. In LREC/COLING, Cited by: cite78†§2 .\nL154:   * Li et al. (2024b) H. Li, Q. Dong, Z. Tang, C. Wang, X. Zhang, H. Huang, S. Huang, X. Huang, Z. Huang, D. Zhang, et al. Synthetic data (almost) from scratch: generalized instruction tuning for language models. Cited by: cite77†§2 .\nL